  "query_preview": "..A large trough is visible in mid-level WV satellite \nimagery, with its axis currently extending across northern \nCalifornia. In general this is introducing drier mid-level air \nthrough southwesterly flow across northern AZ, and will act to \nsubdue thunderstorm activity over the next few days. However \ncurrent mesoanalysis reveals that a plume of higher low-level\nmoisture is being wrapped into the flow and spreading northward. \nA few isolated storms began to fire as of 1 AM MST as this air \nreached Yavapai and Coconino county. These storms will likely \nremain very isolated and continue to propagate northeastward \nthrough the early morning.\n\nInto the daytime today, activity will likely remain similar to \nyesterday. PW is forecast to stay the same or fall slightly from \nyesterday with the continued entrainment of drier air aloft. \nHowever with plenty of lingering surface moisture (dewpoints in \nthe 50s) and likely cloud-free morning skies, modest instability \nwill build and allow for isolated thunderstorms. A few stronger \nthunderstorms will be possible especially towards far northern \nAZ. Storm motion will be fast, which should help to reduce flash \nflood risk unless storms train. The west coast troughing will also\nact to bring breezy southwest winds with gusts up to 30 mph as \nwell as daytime temperatures a few degrees below normal for early \nSeptember. \n\nBy Thursday, the ridge to our east begins to nudge towards us enough \nto steer moisture into far eastern AZ. Higher coverage of \nthunderstorms are possible across the White Mountains on Thursday \nafternoon as a result. Outside of this area, most of northern AZ \nwill remain drier with isolated storms during the day on Thursday.\nInto Thursday evening, a continued surge of moisture is expected into\neastern and central AZ. We may see thunderstorm activity well \ninto Thursday night/Friday morning for these regions, and the \ncurrent forecast maintains a 30-50% chance of precipitation. By \nFriday and into the weekend, the surge of moisture is forecast to \nspread westward. These moisture increases will in part be due to \ninfluences from Tropical Storm Marie. Unfortunately, there is \nuncertainty in the exact track of the tropical system and thus in \nwhere the deepest moisture will land. Regardless, an increase in \nmoisture with widespread 30-60% precipitation changes remains in \nthe forecast each day Saturday through Tuesday. The current \noutlook calls for a stormy holiday weekend, but stay tuned to \nfuture forecasts for any updates.",
